  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/23195f0e8657cfbd219f540d6f4403c6bffe1056849e4da43a11b61f4afba76a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/fad89577df15122a8f80996cbf4f1b840084f51cd93d97a776f5562d59a4cc99.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba5f76185cc6d2e25204c10e8e25b0bf28b3686cb1d494f87af8f03c4b2f2b66.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
